node_modules/
build/
out/
review/
logs/
assets/stock/*
!assets/stock/.gitkeep
!assets/stock/README.md
# Credentials. `.env` alone matched only the literal name, so `.env.production`
# and `.env.local` were tracked-able -- and `.dockerignore` was STRICTER than
# this file, which is backwards: a registry push is optional and a public repo
# is already published. `.env.example` is negated back because it is the
# operator's only list of what the seven variables are called.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# TLS material. The deploy that is next terminates TLS on a small VM, which is
# exactly the workflow that writes a key and a certificate into a working copy.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx

assets/test-photos/*
!assets/test-photos/README.md

# Local tool state. `.gstack/` writes its own `.gitignore` containing `*`, but
# that file is untracked and machine-local, so the protection does not survive
# a fresh clone where a tool recreates the directory before writing it.
.gstack/
.superpowers/

# The same argument one directory over, and it had the same hole. `.claude/`
# holds local agent state -- settings, hooks, worktrees, and whatever a future
# MCP integration writes there. `.claude/worktrees/` was covered ONLY by
# `.git/info/exclude`, which is local and never committed, so a fresh clone had
# no protection and the first broad `git add .` publishes whatever is inside.
# This repo tracks no skills or settings of its own, so nothing here wants
# tracking; if that changes, negate the one path rather than dropping the rule.
.claude/

# Security reviews stay on this machine. This repo is public, and an accurate
# list of a live system's open weaknesses is an attack roadmap. Publish a review
# once its findings are closed, not while they are open.
docs/security-review-*.md
